id=a52sxq_soundtrigger_load_no_reboot
name=Sound trigger: a refused model load no longer restarts the audio HAL, and a busy refusal is retried
author=kusti420
description=Tester log 2026-10-20 (logsrecentes.txt): "Hey Google" (vendor UUID 7038ddc8-30f2-11e6-b0ac-40a8f03d3f15) was loaded while audio played; the A52s HIDL sound trigger HAL took the non-LPI path ('lpi NOT supported due to concurrency') and ape_reg_sm failed to open the ADSP listen session (/dev/snd/pcmC0D10c 'Out of memory', status -19 = -ENODEV, 5 HAL retries). The audio side is the stock A14 one (sound_trigger_platform_info.xml, mixer paths, ACDB, sound_trigger.primary.lahaina.so, q6_dlkm/platform_dlkm all byte-identical to SM-A528B), and stock A14 services.jar maps every load status to HalException and reboots the HAL too, so stock had no deferral either. The 'no LSM table for 602/app_type 3' ACDB lines are not the cause: the HAL's boot probe of the same Google non-LPI usecase prints them and opens pcmC0D10c fine (test phone 2026-10-22), and Bixby (app_type 5) re-registers non-LPI during playback fine. The -ENOMEM comes from the kernel LSM client alloc (no free LSM session / APR port), a resource refusal. 9.3.63: HalException on load -> RecoverableException(INTERNAL_ERROR) instead of SoundTriggerHalEnforcer rebooting the HAL (= the whole audio HAL process: audio + BT audio drop, audioserver restart). Now K4StRetry (src/) reports -ENODEV/-EBUSY/-ENOMEM/-EAGAIN as RESOURCE_CONTENTION, the AOSP status for this: SoundTriggerHelper keeps the model requested, tells the client onRecognitionPaused() and loads it again on onResourcesAvailable(). A HIDL 2.x HAL never sends that callback (AIDL HALs do), so K4StRetry sends it on the HAL stack's global callback when all playback stopped (+5 s for audio HAL output standby) or the screen turned off (+2 s), and now also on a timer: 10 s, 30 s, 1, 2, 5 min after a refusal, then every 10 min while refusals continue (back to 10 s after 15 min without one; no wakelock, a retry is one failed HAL load). Why the timer (test phone 2026-10-24, /mnt/B1/OneUi9/scratch/hotword): the refusal is not a playback concurrency limit. With Google's real DSP model (res/raw/en_all.mmap of the stock HotwordEnrollmentOKGoogleEx3HEXAGON, magic 0x73 like GSA's) loaded through the middleware, the HAL loads and starts it during music playback with the screen on and off (st_hw_check_and_update_lpi: lpi NOT supported due to concurrency -> NON_LPI_BARGE_IN usecase, listen-ape-handset-mic-preproc, lpi_enable=false, ape_start status 0), and an LPI session that is active when playback starts is moved to non-LPI and back by the HAL itself (handle_audio_concurrency pause/resume, no client involvement). The tester log of 2026-10-23 (logs.txt) shows the same "pcmC0D10c: Out of memory" for Bixby's model in LPI mode with nothing playing and for the HAL's boot probe in a freshly started HAL, 191 times over 30 min and 32 HAL restarts. That error is msm_lsm_open -> q6lsm_client_alloc() returning NULL in the kernel (no free LSM session, or the APR LSM service refusing the client port); with 8 Listen front ends and 8 LSM sessions a free front end can only meet it if a kernel LSM session or the APR registration is stuck, which playback or the screen do not change. pcm 10 shows in every refusal because the HAL takes the first free Listen slot; when its boot probe (get_module_version) succeeds that slot is never released (QTI st-hal ape_close_session does not free the pcm id), so a healthy phone loads on pcm 16 (Listen 2). The earlier wording "the A52s chip refuses Google's model during playback, like on stock" was wrong. Other load statuses stay INTERNAL_ERROR. A HAL status refusal of a load never reboots: the enforcer records a model only after a successful load, so a refusal leaves nothing to resync. A load that fails otherwise (not a HalException) or hangs past the 3 s watchdog deadline is handled like any other HAL error. Every other HAL error is handled by soundtrigger_no_hal_reboot through the same K4StRetry (outside calls: reboot after a 3 s grace with the mode re-read; during calls - audio mode, telephony call state of any SIM, or voice/ringtone playback or voice recording - after the call). onResourcesAvailable runs on its own thread (K4StRetryCb), never on the thread that decides reboots, because it takes the SoundTriggerModule monitor that a hung HAL call holds. The screen-off / audio-stopped retry holds a partial wakelock for delay + 1 s. K4StRetry forgets a HAL stack when it is detached (SoundTriggerHalEnforcer.detach, called from SoundTriggerModule.binderDied), so dead stacks are not kept or retried. Watchdog timeouts (hung HAL call) are deferred during calls too (soundtrigger_no_hal_reboot). Telephony calls: AOSP/stock DeviceStateHandler unloads sound models for the call and reloads them after it, by design.
